---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ConfidenceTag from "../components/ConfidenceTag.astro";
import { SITE, href } from "../lib/site";
---

<Base title="Methodology" description="Where the numbers come from, how the headline is chosen, and what the rebuild model assumes.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="label">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How the numbers work</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      U.S. munitions inventories are classified. Nobody outside the Pentagon knows the exact count, and this site
      doesn't pretend to. What it does is collect the best public estimates and reports, keep each one tied to its
      source, and show where they agree and where they don't.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources and claims</h2>
    <p>
      Every number starts as a <strong>claim</strong> inside a <strong>report</strong>: a CSIS analysis, a news story,
      an inspector general summary. A claim records the missile, what's being measured (prewar stock, number used,
      number left, share used), the date the number describes, how the source hedged it ("about", "more than",
      "nearly"), and the exact sentence it came from.
    </p>
    <p>Each claim gets one of three tags:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><ConfidenceTag confidence="official" /> On the record from a government body: DoD, an inspector general, CRS, CBO.</li>
      <li>
        <ConfidenceTag confidence="reported" /> Journalists citing officials, documents or unnamed sources. Often the
        freshest information, but usually without a stated baseline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <ConfidenceTag confidence="estimate" /> An analyst's model. Most stock counts here come from CSIS (Mark Cancian
        and Chris Park), who rebuild inventories from DoD budget exhibits: missiles bought (P-5) and delivered (P-21),
        less 5% for testing and training, less earlier wars and transfers to Ukraine.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Quotes are checked by machine</h2>
    <p>
      Before a report is merged, a script downloads the source page and confirms every quote appears in it word for
      word, ignoring only curly-vs-straight quotes and whitespace. A quote that only paraphrases the source fails.
      Sources that can't be fetched automatically (a PDF, or a paywall) are marked for manual checking.
    </p>

    <h2>How the headline is picked</h2>
    <p>For each missile, the big number is the newest stock-left figure we can state <em>without mixing sources</em>:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>A direct "this many left" claim, or</li>
      <li>Prewar stock minus the number used, when the same document gives both.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Ties on date go to the more specific figure: a range beats "about", which beats "at most". The share left is
      measured against the same document's prewar figure when it has one, otherwise the most recent earlier one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some newer reporting can't be converted. CNN's August report that "nearly 80%" of THAAD interceptors had been
      used doesn't say 80% <em>of what</em>, and Pentagon and CSIS baselines differ. Rather than multiplying it against
      someone else's number, the site flags it as <strong>Newer reporting</strong> next to the headline.
    </p>

    <h2>The rebuild model</h2>
    <p>
      Each missile page projects the stockpile day by day from the headline estimate, using sourced inputs listed on
      that page:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Existing orders</strong> arrive at the recent U.S. delivery rate, CSIS's five-year average of
        procurement.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The FY2027 rebuild order</strong> starts arriving on the date in DoD's delivery schedules. It's
        delivered over one production lot, but never faster than announced production capacity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A renewed war</strong> is off by default. Turned on, it fires at the opening campaign's average rate
        (the April ceasefire tally divided by 39 days) unless you change it. Stock can't go below zero; the unmet part is
        reported as a shortfall.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>What it leaves out, on purpose:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Firing since the estimate date.</strong> Fighting resumed on July 8 at lower intensity, but no one has
        published counts for it. Projections that start from April estimates are optimistic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Allied demand.</strong> Announced capacities cover all customers. About half of current Patriot output
        goes abroad.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Later budgets.</strong> Only the FY2027 order is modeled. FY2028 and beyond would speed things up.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The model is deliberately simple, so every input is visible and adjustable. Its results land near CSIS's own
      return-to-prewar dates for Tomahawk, THAAD and Patriot. For JASSM it is slower than CSIS, which expects large
      deliveries from recent orders.
    </p>

    <h2>How updates happen</h2>
    <p>
      A scheduled job checks a fixed list of sources (CSIS, Pentagon contract announcements, news searches for each
      missile) once a day. When something new mentions stockpile numbers, Claude drafts the claims, a script checks
      every quote against the page, and the draft opens as a pull request. A person reviews it before anything goes
      live. Nothing is published straight from an AI.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Spotted a wrong number, a newer source, or a quote out of context? <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new`}
        >Open an issue</a
      >. All data lives in plain JSON in the <a href={SITE.repo}>repository</a>, one file per source.
    </p>
    <p><a href={href("/")}>← Back to the tracker</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin: 40px 0 0;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 44px);
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin: 8px 0 12px;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 18px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 21px;
    margin: 36px 0 8px;
  }
  li {
    margin: 6px 0;
  }
</style>
